'''Macaio''' ({{Langx|yrl|Maçayó|4=springs}}; {{Langx|an|Macau}}) is the most-populous city within the [[Bayara|Bayaran community]] of Balisca, the [[List of largest cities in Balisca|second-most-populous in Balisca]] overall (after [[Castejón]]), and the [[List of largest cities in the Algarves|third-largest city in the Algarves]]. Additionally, is the largest predominantly [[W:Aragonese language|Jacano-speaking]] city in the world. The city is the administrative center of the surrounding [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Carpinchera]], one of the [[List of Baliscan regions by population|most populous]] and [[Social Development Index|socially developed]] regions in Balisca. In 2024, Macaio was ranked as an [[w:global city|Alpha− global city]] (as listed by the [[Adonia Globalization and World Cities Ranks|GaWC]]), exerting considerable influence in {{W|biotechnology}}, {{W|alternative finance}}, [[W:Visual arts|visual arts]] and the [[W:Film industry|film industry]].  

The metropolitan area, [[Greater Macaio]], is the [[List of metropolitan areas in Balisca|third most populous]] in Balisca. The process of [[w:Conurbation|conurbation]] between the metropolitan areas located around the Greater Macaio ([[Porto]], [[Sant Chuan|Sant Chuan d'os Baxes]] and [[San Chorche]]) has created the [[Solana|Solana Megalopolis]], a [[Megaregions of Balisca|megaregion]] with more than 29 million inhabitants. It is the second largest megapolis [[Megaregions of Balisca|in Balisca]], after the [[Concordia]] conurbation, and one of [[List of megalopolises|the world's largest]].

The area of Macaio was previously inhabited by [[Kurupiranga]] peoples, specifically the [[Guarãgua people|Guarãgua]] and [[Ipugua people|Ipugua]] tribes. Fortifications were built by Jacano explorers at [[Punta Lagunas]] in 1527, eventually leading to the founding of the Macaio settlement in 1549. Macaio played a significant role in the exploration and development of the Baliscan interior, as the city set at the mouth of the [[Tapajós River]]. It became the capital of the [[Baliscan War of Independence|revolutionary]] [[United Provinces of Bayara]] in 1810 and joined the [[Northern Confederation]] later that same year. It opposed the [[Xunta Nacionalista|secession of Bayara]] from the confederation in 1826 and briefly served as the capital of the short lived [[Carpinchera Republic]], which sided with [[Baliscan Civil War|confederal forces]] against the secessionists. The city subsequently avoided the confederal occupation imposed on Bayara in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]. Since then, the city has rapidly expanded beyond its original location through land reclamation and [[W:Municipal annexation|municipal annexation]].

It serves as the headquarters to Baliscan entertainment, {{W|shipping industry|shipping}}, and {{W|telecommunications}} entities, including three of the country's major corporations – The [[Baliscan Post]], [[Baliscan Overseas Shipping Company|BOSCO]] and Jacrinada – [[tba]], one of Balisca's largest telemedia cooperative federations. The home of many universities and institutes, it is the second-largest center of {{W|research and development}} in Balisca, accounting for 14% of national scientific output according to 2019 data. Macaio has been ranked first in the evaluation of "Communes with Strongest Social Development Improvement" issued by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS), and third in the evaluation of cities with most sustainable development potential in Abyala. The economic base of the city is {{W|biotechnology}} and {{W|tourism}}.
